  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 258 PM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather is expected to continue through most of next \nweek, though a transition to a more active pattern is possible by \nthe end of the week into next weekend. The synoptic weather pattern \nfor the weak ahead will features a broad ridge over the central US, \nwith another slow moving closed low meandering into the western part \nof the country. Southwest flow aloft will prevail through most of \nthe week. Temperatures will be well above seasonal averages with 700-\nmb temperatures around +5 to +8C for much of the week. Look for \nhighs in the mid 60s to mid 70s across the area, which will be about \n10-15F above normal. The flow direction will continue to improve mid \nto upper level moisture through the week. By Thursday afternoon, \nNAEFS mean precipitable water is actually quite close to \nclimatological maximums. Despite the high moisture content, we don't \nhave much lift to actually translate this into precipitation. Weak \nheating this late in the season will keep convective instability to \na minimum, and synoptic forcing appears unimpressive at this time. \nStill, we may see some widely scattered high-based showers each \nafternoon and early evening through the week ahead. Forcing may be \nstrong enough by Thursday to pick up coverage a little as the upper \nlevel low to our west inches closer. Fortunately, the moisture \nadvection will mix into the boundary layer periodically, which \nshould keep fire weather concerns down. \n\nThe upper level low will make its exit to the east sometime in the \nFriday through Sunday period. This will present the next chance for \nnotable precipitation, while also dropping temperatures closer to \nseasonal normals. Most likely, Friday will be another warm day but \nwith increasing chances for rainfall later in the day, with the cool-\ndown waiting until Saturday. Since we are still 6 to 7 days out, \nprecipitation is highly uncertain at this time. NBM PoPs are \ngenerally in the 30 to 50% range which seems appropriate at this \nlead time. While a sizable minority of ensemble members have \nhealthy precipitation totals (0.5\" or more), this system appears \nfairly disorganized so there is also a possibility (30% or so) that \nit delivers minimal rainfall to our area.",
